1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:04,637
This jar holds every opal
I found in Coober Pedy.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:05,600
Listen.

3
00:00:05,575 --> 00:00:06,725
That's mostly the jar.

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:09,813
Plenty of people in that
desert town live underground.

5
00:00:09,975 --> 00:00:11,250
I went to find out why.

6
00:00:13,476 --> 00:00:16,951
Coober Pedy sits in the
outback of South Australia,

7
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,314
roughly eight hundred and forty-six
kilometres north-west of Adelaide by road.

8
00:00:21,489 --> 00:00:23,826
That's about five hundred
and twenty-five miles.

9
00:00:23,989 --> 00:00:28,489
Estimates vary, but around half the
town lives in homes dug into the hills.

10
00:00:28,651 --> 00:00:32,989
At the twenty twenty-one census, around
fifteen hundred people lived in the town,

11
00:00:33,039 --> 00:00:34,976
depending on how you draw its edges.

12
00:00:35,138 --> 00:00:36,351
I planned to be one of them.

13
00:00:36,513 --> 00:00:39,963
I packed a hat, a lot of
water and a lot of confidence.

14
00:00:40,126 --> 00:00:44,051
By the end of the trip I understood
why people here dig instead of build.

15
00:00:44,226 --> 00:00:46,651
It cost me my dignity and a golf ball.

16
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:52,359
My first morning, I went noodling.

17
00:00:52,521 --> 00:00:56,972
Noodling means sifting through the piles
of waste dirt that miners leave behind,

18
00:00:57,172 --> 00:00:58,459
hoping they missed something.

19
00:00:58,634 --> 00:00:59,972
The piles are everywhere.

20
00:01:00,222 --> 00:01:04,709
Thousands of pale cones of rubble, as
if a giant had been sneezing sand for a

21
00:01:04,759 --> 00:01:05,559
century.

22
00:01:05,722 --> 00:01:07,322
I picked a pile that looked lucky.

23
00:01:07,484 --> 00:01:09,746
It looked exactly like every other pile.

24
00:01:09,909 --> 00:01:10,709
I dug.

25
00:01:10,759 --> 00:01:11,696
I found a rock.

26
00:01:11,859 --> 00:01:12,997
I dug faster.

27
00:01:13,159 --> 00:01:14,809
I found a more confident rock.

28
00:01:14,972 --> 00:01:17,022
Then I tossed a pebble over my shoulder,

29
00:01:17,159 --> 00:01:18,109
and it sparkled.

30
00:01:18,272 --> 00:01:19,509
I did not turn around.

31
00:01:22,236 --> 00:01:24,623
So why build a town
around a pile of gravel?

32
00:01:24,786 --> 00:01:26,223
It started with a teenager.

33
00:01:26,386 --> 00:01:29,061
On the first of February,
nineteen fifteen,

34
00:01:29,211 --> 00:01:32,673
a prospecting party led by
James Hutchison was out here.

35
00:01:32,849 --> 00:01:36,824
His son Willie, aged fourteen or
fifteen depending on the source,

36
00:01:36,874 --> 00:01:38,723
picked up the first piece of opal.

37
00:01:38,886 --> 00:01:40,773
Miners followed within about a year.

38
00:01:40,948 --> 00:01:44,749
Today this field is credited with an
estimated seventy percent of the world's

39
00:01:44,799 --> 00:01:48,023
precious opal, though some
local figures run higher.

40
00:01:48,186 --> 00:01:50,461
And here's the part people
often get half right.

41
00:01:50,624 --> 00:01:54,611
The rock the opal sits in was laid
down by an ancient inland sea.

42
00:01:54,786 --> 00:01:56,811
The opal itself came much later.

43
00:01:56,986 --> 00:02:01,311
One dating study suggests roughly
twelve to sixteen million years ago,

44
00:02:01,436 --> 00:02:03,011
long after the sea had gone.

45
00:02:03,186 --> 00:02:07,011
Picture a layer cake where the jam
was squeezed in years after baking.

46
00:02:07,186 --> 00:02:08,461
The sea baked the cake.

47
00:02:08,636 --> 00:02:10,123
The opal is the late jam.

48
00:02:10,286 --> 00:02:13,073
Whether you need a permit to
noodle depends on who you ask,

49
00:02:13,123 --> 00:02:14,623
so I checked before I dug.

50
00:02:16,357 --> 00:02:17,682
That's piece one of the why.

51
00:02:17,844 --> 00:02:20,194
Opal pulled people into
the middle of nowhere.

52
00:02:20,356 --> 00:02:21,932
It did not tell them where to sleep.

53
00:02:22,106 --> 00:02:23,494
I found that out at lunchtime.

54
00:02:26,712 --> 00:02:27,912
By noon I was melting.

55
00:02:28,087 --> 00:02:32,712
The sky was white, the dirt was hot,
and my hat had quietly resigned.

56
00:02:32,887 --> 00:02:35,487
I wrote in my notebook, in wobbly letters,

57
00:02:35,537 --> 00:02:36,950
I am not moving here.

58
00:02:37,124 --> 00:02:38,399
Then I noticed the doors.

59
00:02:38,562 --> 00:02:40,750
Doors cut straight into the hillsides.

60
00:02:40,924 --> 00:02:43,249
Pipes poking out of the
ground like snorkels.

61
00:02:43,424 --> 00:02:46,275
My first guess was that the
miners were hiding their opal.

62
00:02:46,437 --> 00:02:47,237
Reasonable.

63
00:02:47,362 --> 00:02:48,162
Wrong.

64
00:02:48,124 --> 00:02:50,762
My second guess was that
they simply love caves.

65
00:02:50,924 --> 00:02:52,162
Also wrong, mostly.

66
00:02:52,324 --> 00:02:56,100
I stood there, slowly dripping,
and asked myself the real question.

67
00:02:56,275 --> 00:02:58,762
If it's this hot up here,
what is it like down there?

68
00:03:00,992 --> 00:03:03,304
The reason is boring and brilliant.

69
00:03:03,467 --> 00:03:07,242
A few metres of rock is slow to
heat up and slow to cool down,

70
00:03:07,392 --> 00:03:09,704
so underground the
temperature hardly moves.

71
00:03:09,867 --> 00:03:12,967
NASA puts it at about
twenty-three degrees Celsius,

72
00:03:13,017 --> 00:03:14,905
around seventy-three Fahrenheit.

73
00:03:15,067 --> 00:03:17,855
Residents describe roughly
twenty-four all year,

74
00:03:17,917 --> 00:03:20,292
with no air conditioning and no heater.

75
00:03:20,454 --> 00:03:24,154
I sat on my bag in the shade and
counted that on my fingers twice.

76
00:03:24,317 --> 00:03:26,367
It's the same trick as a wine cellar.

77
00:03:26,542 --> 00:03:28,667
The cellar doesn't make anything cold.

78
00:03:28,842 --> 00:03:30,242
It just ignores the weather.

79
00:03:32,464 --> 00:03:34,951
So I booked a room in
an underground hotel.

80
00:03:35,114 --> 00:03:37,702
I walked down the
corridor, out of the glare,

81
00:03:37,752 --> 00:03:39,214
and my shape came back.

82
00:03:39,389 --> 00:03:42,976
The walls were bare rock,
curving up into a pale ceiling.

83
00:03:43,139 --> 00:03:43,952
No window.

84
00:03:44,114 --> 00:03:45,001
No traffic.

85
00:03:45,164 --> 00:03:46,489
No sun trying to get in.

86
00:03:46,651 --> 00:03:49,027
I slept like a rock, inside a rock.

87
00:03:49,201 --> 00:03:51,989
By breakfast I had crossed
out the line in my notebook.

88
00:03:52,152 --> 00:03:53,639
I was moving here after all.

89
00:03:56,872 --> 00:04:01,322
In the morning I stared at the rock above
my bed and asked the obvious question.

90
00:04:01,509 --> 00:04:02,484
Who digs a bedroom?

91
00:04:02,647 --> 00:04:04,747
Someone who already digs for a living.

92
00:04:04,922 --> 00:04:09,060
According to a state heritage report,
the first miners probably started digging

93
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:11,447
homes within a few years of the find.

94
00:04:11,622 --> 00:04:14,784
The local council even
issues a permit for a blower,

95
00:04:14,909 --> 00:04:17,110
a truck that vacuums dirt out of a tunnel.

96
00:04:17,284 --> 00:04:20,259
That one permit covers mining
and digging out a home.

97
00:04:20,409 --> 00:04:25,210
A mine and a bedroom, it turns out, are
the same hole with different furniture.

98
00:04:26,957 --> 00:04:28,219
Here's how I think about it.

99
00:04:28,394 --> 00:04:33,207
Building a house out here and keeping it
cool is like running a fan every afternoon,

100
00:04:33,332 --> 00:04:34,957
every summer, forever.

101
00:04:35,119 --> 00:04:37,507
Digging one is like
buying a really good hat.

102
00:04:37,670 --> 00:04:40,307
You pay once, and then
the rock does the work.

103
00:04:40,482 --> 00:04:42,645
I couldn't find hard
numbers on the savings,

104
00:04:42,695 --> 00:04:45,869
but locals say they don't need
air conditioning down there.

105
00:04:48,097 --> 00:04:49,109
Then I zoomed out.

106
00:04:49,272 --> 00:04:50,909
One room becomes a house.

107
00:04:51,071 --> 00:04:53,984
Houses become a hotel, shops and churches.

108
00:04:54,159 --> 00:04:58,196
The Serbian Orthodox church of Saint
Elijah was dug into the rock in nineteen

109
00:04:58,246 --> 00:04:59,084
ninety-three.

110
00:04:59,259 --> 00:05:02,796
I stood at the back with my hat
in my hands and kept very quiet.

111
00:05:02,959 --> 00:05:04,672
Even the echo seemed polite.

112
00:05:04,847 --> 00:05:07,359
The town picked its name
around nineteen twenty,

113
00:05:07,422 --> 00:05:09,909
though records differ by a year or two,

114
00:05:09,984 --> 00:05:12,434
and got its own council
in nineteen eighty-seven.

115
00:05:12,597 --> 00:05:15,846
Everyone here had the same
idea, with the same machines.

116
00:05:19,068 --> 00:05:22,305
Late afternoon, when the sun
finally lowered its voice,

117
00:05:22,443 --> 00:05:23,656
I went to play golf.

118
00:05:23,818 --> 00:05:27,243
The Opal Fields Golf Club was
set up in nineteen seventy-six,

119
00:05:27,293 --> 00:05:28,993
and it has no grass at all.

120
00:05:29,168 --> 00:05:31,156
The fairways are white crushed rock.

121
00:05:31,331 --> 00:05:35,968
The greens, which they call scrapes,
are rock dust mixed with waste oil.

122
00:05:36,131 --> 00:05:39,668
Players carry a small square
of artificial turf to hit from.

123
00:05:39,843 --> 00:05:43,018
I carried mine under my arm
like a tiny rolled carpet.

124
00:05:43,193 --> 00:05:45,230
My backpack already has a red one.

125
00:05:45,393 --> 00:05:46,855
Now it had a green one too.

126
00:05:47,018 --> 00:05:49,906
I looked extremely prepared
for a sport I could not play.

127
00:05:52,024 --> 00:05:52,824
Then night.

128
00:05:52,964 --> 00:05:56,974
The heat finally backed off, and the
course changed shape in the dark.

129
00:05:57,244 --> 00:06:01,214
When the days get too hot, people here
play in the dark with balls that glow.

130
00:06:01,504 --> 00:06:03,194
My ball glowed beautifully,

131
00:06:03,504 --> 00:06:05,354
all the way into a pile of dirt.

132
00:06:05,644 --> 00:06:06,444
Gone.

133
00:06:06,324 --> 00:06:08,054
And the strangest detail of all.

134
00:06:08,284 --> 00:06:11,914
This club has a reciprocal arrangement
with St Andrews in Scotland,

135
00:06:12,144 --> 00:06:13,714
often called the home of golf.

136
00:06:14,044 --> 00:06:18,914
Golf Digest reported that nobody knew of
any St Andrews member who had actually come

137
00:06:18,964 --> 00:06:19,764
out to play.

138
00:06:19,924 --> 00:06:20,724
I get it.

139
00:06:20,724 --> 00:06:23,534
I lost a ball and my
dignity here, in that order.

140
00:06:27,276 --> 00:06:30,406
Back on my map page, the
costs started piling up.

141
00:06:30,616 --> 00:06:33,146
The drive from Adelaide
takes about nine hours.

142
00:06:33,516 --> 00:06:35,206
Water isn't around the corner either.

143
00:06:35,476 --> 00:06:39,206
The town pumps it from an underground
bore about sixty metres deep,

144
00:06:39,356 --> 00:06:42,406
some twenty-three to twenty-five
kilometres to the north-east,

145
00:06:42,696 --> 00:06:44,966
depending on which
council document you read.

146
00:06:45,176 --> 00:06:47,606
Then it filters it by reverse osmosis,

147
00:06:47,656 --> 00:06:49,946
which it has done since
nineteen eighty-five.

148
00:06:49,996 --> 00:06:54,266
According to an older report, the ageing
pipes were losing somewhere between thirty

149
00:06:54,316 --> 00:06:56,226
and fifty percent of that water.

150
00:06:56,496 --> 00:07:00,966
The council has also been the only one in
South Australia running its own water and

151
00:07:01,016 --> 00:07:01,816
power.

152
00:07:01,816 --> 00:07:06,266
In twenty twenty-six, the water service
was moving over to the state utility,

153
00:07:06,376 --> 00:07:07,176
SA Water.

154
00:07:07,416 --> 00:07:10,526
Imagine your kitchen tap
connected to a straw that long.

155
00:07:12,816 --> 00:07:13,966
Then there are the holes.

156
00:07:14,129 --> 00:07:17,504
Old mine shafts sit all
around town, some unmarked,

157
00:07:17,554 --> 00:07:21,003
some hidden under sheets of
iron or mesh or a heap of dirt.

158
00:07:21,166 --> 00:07:23,253
Nobody seems to have an exact count.

159
00:07:23,429 --> 00:07:26,366
One local rescuer, according to the ABC,

160
00:07:26,429 --> 00:07:28,228
put it at around two million.

161
00:07:28,391 --> 00:07:31,678
Warning signs stand at the
entrances to the opal fields,

162
00:07:31,728 --> 00:07:34,954
and I read every single
one walking backwards.

163
00:07:35,129 --> 00:07:36,191
That was a mistake.

164
00:07:36,353 --> 00:07:41,141
In February twenty twenty-four, a man
looking for opal at night with an ultraviolet

165
00:07:41,191 --> 00:07:44,791
torch fell about twenty-five
metres down an old shaft.

166
00:07:44,953 --> 00:07:50,366
After that fall, SafeWork SA put out an
alert called Mind your step in opal fields.

167
00:07:50,541 --> 00:07:52,504
Searching at night raises the risk.

168
00:07:52,678 --> 00:07:55,691
Standing at the edge of one
hole, for about a minute,

169
00:07:55,841 --> 00:07:57,154
I was ready to go home.

170
00:08:00,784 --> 00:08:04,414
On my last evening I drove
north to Kanku-Breakaways,

171
00:08:04,464 --> 00:08:06,654
about twenty-five kilometres out of town.

172
00:08:06,864 --> 00:08:07,894
I left the hat in the car.

173
00:08:07,944 --> 00:08:11,294
Flat-topped hills, banded white, yellow,

174
00:08:11,504 --> 00:08:12,974
orange and deep red.

175
00:08:13,204 --> 00:08:17,914
This is the country of the Antakirinja
Matu-Yankunytjatjara people.

176
00:08:18,184 --> 00:08:21,534
The whole park is a registered
Aboriginal heritage site,

177
00:08:21,684 --> 00:08:24,694
and visitors are asked not
to touch or take anything.

178
00:08:24,904 --> 00:08:30,712
It belongs to their corporation and has
been jointly managed with the state since

179
00:08:30,762 --> 00:08:31,562
twenty thirteen.

180
00:08:30,859 --> 00:08:34,394
In twenty eleven, the Federal Court
recognised their native title over nearly

181
00:08:34,444 --> 00:08:38,214
seventy-nine thousand square
kilometres around Coober Pedy.

182
00:08:38,364 --> 00:08:40,714
The court sat in Coober Pedy to do it.

183
00:08:40,844 --> 00:08:42,294
According to the determination,

184
00:08:42,584 --> 00:08:44,214
some town blocks were left out.

185
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:50,328
Even the town's name is usually
said to come from Aboriginal words,

186
00:08:50,378 --> 00:08:52,153
and nobody fully agrees on it.

187
00:08:52,328 --> 00:08:55,590
Some sources translate them as
something like white man's hole,

188
00:08:55,727 --> 00:08:56,965
others as water hole.

189
00:08:57,128 --> 00:08:59,765
Linguists still debate
where the words came from.

190
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:02,990
I put my notebook away and
watched the hills turn purple.

191
00:09:03,165 --> 00:09:05,527
The miners arrived more
than a century ago.

192
00:09:05,690 --> 00:09:07,940
This land had its people long before that.

193
00:09:11,177 --> 00:09:12,689
So here's how the jar adds up.

194
00:09:12,852 --> 00:09:14,651
Opal brought people to the desert.

195
00:09:14,814 --> 00:09:16,601
The heat pushed them into the rock.

196
00:09:16,764 --> 00:09:20,539
And the digging skills they already had
made a hole cheaper than fighting the sun.

197
00:09:20,701 --> 00:09:25,114
Down there, according to NASA, the
rock holds around twenty-three degrees.

198
00:09:25,277 --> 00:09:27,589
Residents say closer to twenty-four.

199
00:09:27,752 --> 00:09:28,652
Would I move there?

200
00:09:28,814 --> 00:09:30,789
Above ground, no chance.

201
00:09:30,964 --> 00:09:32,489
Below ground, absolutely.

202
00:09:32,651 --> 00:09:36,239
My verdict is WOULD MOVE, as
long as the bedroom is a hole.

203
00:09:36,401 --> 00:09:40,764
It suits people who love quiet,
steady temperatures and long drives.

204
00:09:40,927 --> 00:09:43,314
It does not suit anyone
who needs a window,

205
00:09:43,414 --> 00:09:44,264
or a lawn.

206
00:09:44,426 --> 00:09:46,339
I gave the jar one last shake.

207
00:09:46,514 --> 00:09:47,639
Still mostly jar.

208
00:09:47,801 --> 00:09:51,601
Would you give up every window you own for
a room that stays in the low twenties all

209
00:09:51,651 --> 00:09:52,451
year?
